// brotlicontext.nv — which prefix code to use for the next symbol,
// given the two bytes before it.
//
// THIS IS WHAT BROTLI HAS THAT DEFLATE DOES NOT.  A deflate stream has
// one literal code per block.  A Brotli meta-block has up to 256, and
// which one decodes the next literal depends on the two PREVIOUS
// bytes of output through a context mode and a context map.  That is
// most of the format's advantage on text: after `<`, the next byte is
// overwhelmingly a letter; after a digit, another digit; and a code
// per context spends far fewer bits than one code for all of them.
//
// FOUR MODES, AND THE MODE IS PER BLOCK TYPE.  `LSB6` and `MSB6` take
// six bits of the previous byte; `UTF8` is a small classification of
// the previous two bytes designed for text; `SIGNED` is for numeric
// data.  The mode is declared once per literal block type, so a
// meta-block that switches block types switches context modes with it
// — and a decoder that read the mode once decodes the second block
// type's literals with the first one's contexts.
//
// AND THE MAP IS RUN-LENGTH CODED WITH AN INVERSE MOVE-TO-FRONT PASS.
// The context map is up to 64 KiB of small integers, so it is written
// compressed: a prefix code, run-length zeroes, and then optionally
// an inverse MTF over the result.  The MTF flag is one bit and
// forgetting it produces a map that is a permutation of the right one
// — which decodes to plausible bytes and no error.
//
// DISTANCES HAVE CONTEXTS TOO, and a smaller one: four contexts from
// the copy length. Same map machinery, different alphabet, and the
// two are separate calls so a caller cannot pass one map where the
// other belongs.

use brotlierror
use brotliprefix
use brotlistream

// How the two previous bytes become a context number.
pub enum BroContextMode
    // The low six bits of the previous byte.
    BroLsb6
    // The high six bits of the previous byte.
    BroMsb6
    // A classification of the previous two bytes for text: which of
    // several classes the last byte falls in, combined with whether
    // the one before it was a space or a punctuation mark.  The mode a
    // web page's literals actually use.
    BroUtf8
    // The previous two bytes read as a small signed quantity, for
    // numeric and binary data.
    BroSigned

// A context map: one prefix-code index per context.
//
// A struct rather than a bare `[Int]` because the number of trees is a
// fact about the map that a caller validating a block-type switch
// needs, and because it is what bounds the index a corrupt stream can
// name.
pub struct BroContextMap
    // One tree index per context, `context_count` entries long.
    tree_of: [Int]
    // How many distinct prefix codes the map refers to.  A stream that
    // names more than this in the map is `BroBadContextMap`.
    tree_count: Int
    // Whether the stream applied the inverse move-to-front pass.  Kept
    // so a tool can report it and a re-encoder can reproduce the
    // stream byte for byte.
    used_mtf: Bool
    // How many bits the map occupied in the stream it was read from, or
    // 0 for a map that was built.  What `map_bits` answers.
    bits: Int

// ── Internals ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

